## Quality Gates (Go / No-Go)

### Architecture (Clean Architecture / Ports & Adapters)

- [ ] `domain` has no import from `application`, `infrastructure`, `javax.persistence`, or Spring Web annotations
- [ ] Repository has an interface in `domain` and implementation in `infrastructure`
- [ ] Each UseCase resolves a single business action
- [ ] `Controller` only talks to a `UseCase` interface (no direct `Repository`/JPA access)
- [ ] Code placed in the correct layer (`domain`/`application`/`infrastructure`)

### Conventions

- [ ] Error messages centralized as i18n keys (`messages.properties`), never hardcoded
- [ ] New persisted fields/tables have a corresponding Flyway migration
- [ ] Bean Validation used for input validation (`Request`), not manual `if` checks in the `Controller`
- [ ] Naming follows `code-conventions.mdc` (Entity/UseCase/Service/Repository/Model suffixes)

### Tests

- [ ] Service (business logic) validated in unit tests with the Repository interface mocked (Mockito)
- [ ] Controller behavior validated with `@WebMvcTest` + `MockMvc` (UseCase mocked)
- [ ] Repository implementation validated with `@DataJpaTest` or integration test against a real/in-memory database
- [ ] Validation annotations (custom constraints) covered by dedicated validator tests

No-Go if any mandatory item above is unresolved.

---

## Quick Anti-Patterns

- Business logic inside a `Controller` or a `Request`/`Response` DTO
- `domain` importing from `application`/`infrastructure` (breaks the dependency rule)
- Calling `JpaRepository`/`Model` directly from a `Service`, bypassing the `Repository` interface
- Hardcoded error messages/strings instead of i18n keys
- Editing an already-applied Flyway migration instead of creating a new one
- Testing business logic only via controller tests (without dedicated `Service`/UseCase unit tests)

